WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.340
Hey, this is interesting.

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:04.730
Um, I've talked before about a tool called
the d feasibility tests

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:07.169
that can be a useful tool when we're talking
about politics.

00:00:07.169 --> 00:00:12.420
And I first heard about it, uh, I believe
from Peter Bogosian and then I learned about

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.769
some other versions of it from others.

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:20.279
And the idea is to interject and sort of slow
down a conversation that might be going nowhere

00:00:20.279 --> 00:00:26.570
or where you're not likely to make any progress
by asking what evidence would make you change

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:27.910
your position.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:33.450
So if we were debating abortion, for example,
I might say to someone who is an anti choicer

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:39.310
against abortion, what evidence, if I could
present it to you, would get you to change

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:41.310
your mind about this issue.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:45.710
And they might say, well, if you could prove
a, you know, a fetus isn't a person or they

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.380
might say, if I learned God didn't exist,
then I might change my mind about abortion.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:55.320
It's not so much about what the answer is
specifically, but about getting them to think

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.930
like is there actually something that would
change your mind or are you irrationally tied

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:01.320
to this idea?

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.000
Are you tied to this idea?

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.840
Not because of anything factual, so evidence
doesn't even really matter.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:13.149
That's the sort of gist of the defensibility
test and CNN host John Berman ran the defensibility

00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:18.329
tests on former Trump communications director
Anthony Scaramucci and Scaramucci completely

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:22.729
imploded in the sense that he rambled for
minute after minute after minute not answering

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:23.729
the question.

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:29.679
The question was what could Trump do to make
you Anthony Scaramucci stop supporting him

00:01:29.679 --> 00:01:33.970
and look at how long, like I'm not even going
to play it all because it goes on so long.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:38.130
Look at how long Scaramucci just rambles on
without actually answering the question here.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.679
As we sit here this morning, do you still
support president Trump?

00:01:40.679 --> 00:01:41.679
Yeah, of course.

00:01:41.679 --> 00:01:42.729
I support each Ya.

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:44.789
I'm a very John, I'm a very loyal guy.

00:01:44.789 --> 00:01:46.920
I remember I got fired two years ago.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:47.920
Loyalty.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:48.920
Loyalty is one thing.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.479
But then the question is, Anthony, then if
the answer is yes, I own, who's still still

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:55.150
where the president this morning, what would
it take?

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:56.460
What would it take for you?

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.889
I do to stop supporting the president.

00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:07.090
I, well, I, I think he's going in a direction,
and I said this a couple of weeks ago on your

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:11.700
air is going in a direction where there's
a very large group of people that actually

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:17.260
want to support him or actually begging him
to give us reasons to support you.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:22.000
A, he's going in a direction where I guess
he doesn't want that support or he wants people

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.920
to have this twisted like a pretzel loyalty
tests with him where he's going to do things

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.430
that are not making any sense.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.000
But I want you to go out there and sorta defend
it.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Okay.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.120
And so I, I think there's a large group of
people that are going to stop doing that.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.610
And then eventually people will say, look,
I'm sorry, the value system and the rhetoric

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:44.130
does not outweigh the policies.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.130
Okay.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:49.320
And so at some point that will happen and
has not happened for me yet, but there's a

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:50.320
lot of us out here.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.110
It's not, it's not just me.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:57.340
I'm a little bit more vocal, perhaps a tinge
more courageous to admit it, but trust me,

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:00.060
it's former White House administration officials.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.090
It's former cabinet members.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.120
It's former people in the American military.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.211
It's not, it's not just me.

00:03:05.211 --> 00:03:06.790
It's not like I'm the only one.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:11.030
I may be the only one saying it, but trust
me, there's a very large group of people.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:16.750
If he keeps it up, they'll say, okay, the
policies, they're great, but you know why?

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:22.440
This sort of rhetoric and this sort of disunity
sort of stuff is over powering the policy,

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.210
so Scaramucci has no answer.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:28.700
He just repeated that confusing thing over
and over again and he's reduced to sort of

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.060
a blubbering and stuttering mess.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.030
Really nicely done by John Berman.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.940
Not that it's that complex of a technique.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.600
You just say, Oh, what would get you to change
your mind about that?

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:44.780
Now it's common that in many situations, if
you asked this to someone, what evidence,

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:48.670
if I could present it to you, would it would
change your mind?

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:53.130
Very often people don't immediately have an
answer, but sometimes again, the point is

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:59.640
not to have an answer, but to get the person
thinking about whether they are blindly or

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:03.740
irrationally attached to a position for lots
of these Trumpists.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.220
The answer actually is to this question.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:07.220
Nothing.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.470
Nothing would get them to reconsider their
support.

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:11.650
For Donald Trump, I had a funny color.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:15.450
Must've been maybe like a year ago, might've
even been a year and a half ago on the show.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:20.889
Big Trump is who started by saying to me on
the phone, if Trump doesn't build the wall

00:04:20.889 --> 00:04:26.590
during his first term, he doesn't have my
support in 2020 and within about a minute.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:29.320
I said much more concretely.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:35.360
So you said if Trump does not build the wall
before 2020 you don't vote for him in 2020

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.360
is that right?

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.550
And the Trump has on the phone immediately
started hedging and saying, well, I mean it

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.360
depends on why he doesn't build it.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.460
If he doesn't build it because Democrats get
in the way, then that's different.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.340
It sorta depends and then we very quickly
figured out actually there really wasn't anything

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:53.849
that would stop him from voting for Trump
in 2020 because any failure of Trump or apparent

00:04:53.849 --> 00:04:58.940
failure of Trump, he was going to after the
fact that tribute a blame for to someone else.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.990
In the case of the wall it was going to be
Democrats.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:07.419
So this is a really good question for exposing
dogmatic ideologues and then another one that

00:05:07.419 --> 00:05:08.919
can be productive.

00:05:08.919 --> 00:05:13.570
This one, my friend Jay Tomlinson hosted the
best of the left podcast told me about this

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:14.570
one.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:18.800
Why do you think I hold this position on a
particular issue?

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:25.860
So it's much harder for people to say your
position is basically because you're a moron

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.860
that you've come to this conclusion or you're
a bad person.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:33.590
When you're starting to have a conversation
about an issue, you actually say to someone,

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:35.949
okay, let's talk about healthcare.

00:05:35.949 --> 00:05:39.849
You know my position before we start talking
about it.

00:05:39.849 --> 00:05:44.110
Why do you think I have the position I have
about healthcare?

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:50.610
And it is much more difficult for people to
attribute bad faith when you actually say

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.409
to them, tell me how you think I arrived at
my conclusion.

00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:58.370
Uh, if you've had success with either or both
of these techniques, let me know, maybe during

00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.880
live calls later on this week.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.719
Uh, I would love to hear from, from folks
who have actually tried out some of these

00:06:03.719 --> 00:06:03.739
tactics.

